    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d161-a2">

                <pb facs="0912" n="885" sameAs="frus:frus1989-92v31/pg885"/>


                <head>Statement Prepared in the Department of State<anchor n="59" xml:id="d161-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian">undated</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>As we have indicated in the past the United States attaches great importance to
                    the role played by the United Kingdom’s independent nuclear deterrent in helping
                    to maintain world peace.</p>
                <p>As a result, the United States has engaged in a broad pattern of cooperation with
                    the United Kingdom for many years to help maintain and modernize that deterrent.
                    This is what we have referred to as “the existing pattern of cooperation”
                    between the United States and the United Kingdom. It currently includes the
                    provision by the United States to the United Kingdom of Trident SLBMs.</p>
                <p>The United States notes the statements made by British Foreign Secretary Hurd to
                    Soviet Foreign Minister Shevardnadze regarding the future British plans for
                    United Kingdom nuclear forces: “The British strategic force will remain a
                    minimum one in no way comparable to the nuclear forces of the Soviet Union and
                    the United States.”</p>
                <p>We believe this statement with regard to the United Kingdom’s independent nuclear
                    deterrent should satisfy the Soviet Union’s concerns.</p>
            </div>
